WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.652
The first lesson in M3 is not really about memorizing an equation.

2
00:00:05.652 --> 00:00:11.305
It is about learning how to read an energy story.

3
00:00:11.305 --> 00:00:16.957
Energy sits in stores such as Height Store or Motion Store.

4
00:00:16.957 --> 00:00:22.609
It moves by hand-offs from machines or forces.

5
00:00:22.609 --> 00:00:28.262
And whenever a process is not perfectly useful, some of the input ends up on the Leak Trail.

6
00:00:28.262 --> 00:00:33.914
That is why the ledger matters.

7
00:00:33.914 --> 00:00:39.566
A balanced ledger turns energy from a slogan into a traceable story.

8
00:00:39.566 --> 00:00:45.218
The worked example becomes calmer once the learner stops saying energy was used up and starts saying where the input went.

9
00:00:45.218 --> 00:00:50.871
Keep this takeaway in view: M3 begins with a bookkeeping habit: name where the energy is, how it moved, and where any losses went.

10
00:00:50.871 --> 00:00:56.523
What the pod has means stores.

11
00:00:56.523 --> 00:01:02.175
Height Store and Motion Store are energy held in different ways.

12
00:01:02.175 --> 00:01:07.828
How energy moves means hand-offs.

13
00:01:07.828 --> 00:01:13.480
A machine or interaction transfers energy into or out of a store.

14
00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:19.616
This diagram matters because it stops the lesson from drifting into abstract talk.

15
00:01:19.616 --> 00:01:25.752
On the board, the pod can gain Height Store, gain Motion Store, receive input from a machine, and still send part of that input into the Leak Trail.

16
00:01:25.752 --> 00:01:31.888
That means every event in the lesson can be read like an account statement.

17
00:01:31.888 --> 00:01:38.024
What came in?

18
00:01:38.024 --> 00:01:44.160
What became useful?

19
00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:50.296
What leaked away?

20
00:01:50.296 --> 00:01:56.432
Once students see the whole world on one board, the idea of balancing the energy ledger becomes visual instead of purely verbal.

21
00:01:56.432 --> 00:02:02.568
Keep this takeaway in view: The energy ledger becomes reliable when the whole mission is read as stores, transfers, and leaks on one board.

22
00:02:02.568 --> 00:02:08.704
Store panel: Stores describe where the energy is being held at that moment.

23
00:02:08.704 --> 00:02:14.840
Transfer panel: Hand-offs explain how energy changes location or form across the mission.

24
00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:20.115
A good analogy here is a budget ledger.

25
00:02:20.115 --> 00:02:25.390
Money comes in, part is spent on something useful, part may be lost to fees, and the total still has to balance.

26
00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:30.665
Energy questions behave in a similar way.

27
00:02:30.665 --> 00:02:35.940
The input is not allowed to disappear as a vague statement like it got used.

28
00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:41.215
It must be assigned to useful gain or to a loss path such as the Leak Trail.

29
00:02:41.215 --> 00:02:46.490
This analogy is helpful because it changes student language.

30
00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:51.765
Once the learner starts thinking like an accountant rather than like a guesser, the whole module becomes easier to manage.

31
00:02:51.765 --> 00:02:57.040
Keep this takeaway in view: Ledger thinking replaces the weak phrase used up with a much stronger question: where did the energy go?

32
00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:02.315
Income means input hand-off.

33
00:03:02.315 --> 00:03:07.590
The machine or process supplies the total energy entering the event.

34
00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:12.865
Useful spending means store gain.

35
00:03:12.865 --> 00:03:18.140
This is the part that actually becomes the desired energy change.

36
00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:23.241
Two equations anchor this lesson.

37
00:03:23.241 --> 00:03:28.343
The first says total input energy equals useful output energy plus wasted energy.

38
00:03:28.343 --> 00:03:33.444
That is the ledger balance statement.

39
00:03:33.444 --> 00:03:38.546
The second says change in energy equals energy transferred.

40
00:03:38.546 --> 00:03:43.647
That is the hand-off statement.

41
00:03:43.647 --> 00:03:48.749
These formulas matter, but only if students already know what the symbols refer to in the mission story.

42
00:03:48.749 --> 00:03:53.850
The formula is not the story.

43
00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:58.951
The formula is the compressed record of the story.

44
00:03:58.951 --> 00:04:04.053
Keep this takeaway in view: The ledger equations are strongest when they are attached to a named energy story rather than to raw numbers alone.

45
00:04:04.053 --> 00:04:09.154
Balance rule: The useful part and the wasted part together must account for the whole input.

46
00:04:09.154 --> 00:04:14.256
Transfer rule: A hand-off is the thing that changes the amount of energy in a store.

47
00:04:14.256 --> 00:04:19.357
total input energy = useful output energy + wasted energy.

48
00:04:19.357 --> 00:04:24.459
change in energy = energy transferred.

49
00:04:24.459 --> 00:04:29.560
Use the equation after you have identified the stores, the hand-off, and the Leak Trail.

50
00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:35.309
A reliable pattern for Lesson 1 is to ask three questions in order.

51
00:04:35.309 --> 00:04:41.058
What store is changing?

52
00:04:41.058 --> 00:04:46.807
What hand-off caused that change?

53
00:04:46.807 --> 00:04:52.556
And if the useful gain is smaller than the input, where is the Leak Trail?

54
00:04:52.556 --> 00:04:58.305
This simple routine is powerful because it forces the learner to read the physics before choosing any arithmetic.

55
00:04:58.305 --> 00:05:04.055
Once those three answers are visible, the ledger equation becomes almost inevitable.

56
00:05:04.055 --> 00:05:09.804
Keep this takeaway in view: Once the student uses the same three questions every time, the energy ledger becomes much less slippery.

57
00:05:09.804 --> 00:05:15.553
Question one means which store changed?.

58
00:05:15.553 --> 00:05:21.302
Identify whether the story is about Height Store, Motion Store, or another energy destination.

59
00:05:21.302 --> 00:05:27.051
Question two means what hand-off caused it?.

60
00:05:27.051 --> 00:05:32.800
Machines, forces, or processes move energy into or out of the store.

61
00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:36.374
Try four checks.

62
00:05:36.374 --> 00:05:39.948
If useful gain falls while input stays fixed, what must happen to the Leak Trail?

63
00:05:39.948 --> 00:05:43.522
It must rise.

64
00:05:43.522 --> 00:05:47.096
Are stores the same thing as hand-offs?

65
00:05:47.096 --> 00:05:50.670
No.

66
00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:54.244
Is wasted energy missing from the story?

67
00:05:54.244 --> 00:05:57.818
No, it still has to be tracked.

68
00:05:57.818 --> 00:06:01.392
And why must the ledger balance?

69
00:06:01.392 --> 00:06:04.966
Because the full input must be accounted for as useful gain or loss.

70
00:06:04.966 --> 00:06:08.540
Those checks are the core of the first lesson.

71
00:06:08.540 --> 00:06:12.114
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: the student can now read the whole energy story before touching the calculation.

72
00:06:12.114 --> 00:06:15.688
Fixed input means bigger leak.

73
00:06:15.688 --> 00:06:19.262
A smaller useful share means a larger Leak Trail if the total input does not change.

74
00:06:19.262 --> 00:06:22.836
Store versus hand-off means not the same.

75
00:06:22.836 --> 00:06:26.410
A store is what the pod has; a hand-off is how the energy moves.

76
00:06:26.410 --> 00:06:31.290
You are ready for the worked example now.

77
00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:36.170
Start by identifying the input hand-off.

78
00:06:36.170 --> 00:06:41.050
Then name the useful store gain and any Leak Trail.

79
00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:45.930
Write the ledger sentence in words first, then translate it into numbers and equations.

80
00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:50.810
If the story is still unclear, do not calculate yet.

81
00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:55.690
M3 rewards students who can tell the energy story before they start substituting.

82
00:06:55.690 --> 00:07:00.570
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: the ledger language is now strong enough to support the first full M3 calculation.

83
00:07:00.570 --> 00:07:05.450
Step 1: Mark the total energy entering the mission step.

84
00:07:05.450 --> 00:07:10.330
Step 2: Separate useful gain from any wasted path before calculating.

85
00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:15.210
identify the input hand-off.

86
00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:20.090
The strongest M3 answers start from the story of where the energy went.
